.TH "HTMLCANVASELEMENT.CONTEXTRESTORED_EVENT" "3JS" "October 27, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
HTMLCanvasElement.contextrestored_event \- HTMLCanvasElement:
contextrestored event
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]contextrestored\f[B]\f[R] event of the Canvas API is
fired if the user agent restores the backing storage for a
\f[CR]CanvasRenderingContext2D\f[R].
.PP
You can redraw, re\-retrieve resources, and reinitialize the state of
your context after receiving this event.
.SH SYNTAX
Use the event name in \f[CR]addEventListener()\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
addEventListener(\(dqcontextrestored\(dq, (event) => { })
.EE
.PP
Alternatively, assign to the event handler property of the interface:
.IP
.EX
oncontextrestored = (event) => { }
.EE
.SH EVENT TYPE
A generic \f[CR]Event\f[R].
.SH EXAMPLE
The code fragment below detects the context restored event.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] canvas = document.getElementById(\(dqcanvas\(dq);

canvas.addEventListener(\(dqcontextrestored\(dq, (e) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  console.log(e);
  \f[I]// call to redrawCanvas() or similar\f[R]
});
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]HTMLCanvasElement\f[R]: \f[CR]contextlost\f[R] event
